    "summary": "High‑energy Estimated Prophet and Hell In A Bucket with crisp SBD",
    "blurb": "The second set peaks with a fiery Estimated Prophet followed by a high‑octane Hell In A Bucket and fast Eyes Of The World. First‑set highlights include West L.A. Fadeaway and Desolation Row. Jerry’s tone is spot‑on while Brent delivers dazzling keyboards.",
    "review": "May 10, 1986 at the Frost Amphitheatre captured the Dead in classic mid‑80s form: a tight first set that swings from the upbeat groove of West L.A. Fadeaway into the haunting storytelling of Desolation Row, then rolls through Tennessee Jed and New Minglewood Blues before settling into the rolling momentum of Let It Grow. The second set ignites with Hell In A Bucket, launches into a rare Sugaree, and then erupts on an inspired Estimated Prophet that showcases Jerry’s “perfect” tone and Bob’s vocal catharsis. Eyes Of The World follows at breakneck speed, keeping the energy high despite a few listeners noting it felt rushed. Brent’s keyboards are described as “unbelievable ivories,” and Mickey’s drumming, though occasionally dropping the beat—a known era quirk—still drives the groove forward. Overall the show delivers strong improvisational peaks and solid songcraft, making it a standout entry for 1986 fans.\n\nThe audience‑recorded version is also praised for its crystal‑clear capture of the live vibe, but the soundboard tape (gd86-05-10.sbd.hinko.19962.sbeok.shnf) remains the definitive listening experience thanks to its excellent balance and lack of dropouts.",
